Final answer trimmed to under 130 words:

(1) Sequential N+1 / 2N query anti-pattern: per-id awaited `findFirst` + `findMany` inside a `for` loop.

(2) Scales badly: DB round-trips and wall time grow as O(N) and stay serial, so pool use, latency, and lock/parse cost explode as `routineIds` lengthens under load.

(3) Bound query count—batch then assemble (or one windowed SQL):

```ts
async function loadRoutineCards(ids: string[]) {
  if (!ids.length) return [];
  const routineList = await db.query.routines.findMany({
    where: inArray(routines.id, ids),
  });
  const allRuns = await db.query.runs.findMany({
    where: inArray(runs.routineId, ids),
    orderBy: [desc(runs.createdAt)],
  });
  const by = new Map<string, typeof allRuns>();
  for (const r of allRuns) {
    const b = by.get(r.routineId) ?? [];
    if (b.length < 5) { b.push(r); by.set(r.routineId, b); }
  }
  return routineList.map(routine => ({
    routine,
    runs: by.get(routine.id) ?? [],
  }));
}
```

Two queries total (or one `ROW_NUMBER()` CTE for top-5-per-group).

(1) **SQL injection** (string-interpolated user input into raw SQL). Exploit: `x' OR '1'='1' --`

(2) **Worst case on a shared multi-OpCo DB:** attacker can read/modify/delete *all* tenants’ rows (or worse via stacked/UNION queries)—cross-OpCo data theft, sabotage, or full DB compromise under the app’s DB role—not just their own routine.

(3) **Fix — parameterized query:**
```ts
const rows = await db.execute(
  `SELECT id, schedule, last_run FROM routines WHERE name = $1`,
  [name]
);
```
The value is bound as data, never parsed as SQL, so metacharacters cannot change the query structure.

(1) N+1 / 2N sequential query problem: one `findFirst` + one `findMany` per id, each `await`ed in the loop.

(2) Cost is ~2N DB round-trips in series. Latency grows linearly with |routineIds|; under load connection pool and RTT dominate, so throughput collapses as N grows.

(3) Bound to two queries, then join in memory:

```ts
async function loadRoutineCards(routineIds: string[]) {
  if (!routineIds.length) return [];
  const routineRows = await db.query.routines.findMany({
    where: inArray(routines.id, routineIds),
  });
  const runRows = await db.query.runs.findMany({
    where: inArray(runs.routineId, routineIds),
    // orderBy createdAt desc if needed; trim to 5 per id below
  });
  const runsByRoutine = new Map<string, typeof runRows>();
  for (const r of runRows) {
    const list = runsByRoutine.get(r.routineId) ?? [];
    if (list.length < 5) list.push(r);
    runsByRoutine.set(r.routineId, list);
  }
  return routineRows.map((routine) => ({
    routine,
    runs: runsByRoutine.get(routine.id) ?? [],
  }));
}
```

(Or one query with a relational `with: { runs: { limit: 5 } }` if the ORM supports it.)

(1) SQL injection (CWE-89) via string-interpolated user input. Exploit: `x' UNION SELECT id::text, email, secret FROM opco_credentials--`

(2) Worst case on a shared multi-OpCo DB: full cross-tenant read/write (dump other OpCos’ routines, creds, PII), schema damage, or privilege abuse—breaks tenant isolation.

(3) Parameterize; never concatenate:

```ts
const rows = await db.execute(
  `SELECT id, schedule, last_run FROM routines WHERE name = $1`,
  [name]
);
```

Why: the driver binds `name` as data, not SQL text, so metacharacters cannot alter the statement structure or inject extra clauses.

(1) Defects: Timezone — window is built as UTC midnights (`…Z`), not Europe/Dublin local midnights (IST, UTC+1 in summer). Boundary — closed `lte` end at `T23:59:59Z` (second resolution) drops sub-second times after `:59.000` and is not half-open to next midnight.

(2) Mis-bucketing (Dublin UTC+1): A run at local 00:30 on day D is 23:30 UTC on D−1 → lands in D−1’s report, missing from D. Runs 23:00–23:59 UTC on D are local 00:00–00:59 on D+1 → counted on D, missing from D+1. Fractional seconds past `23:59:59.000Z` can fall in neither day.

(3) Corrected window (half-open, Dublin):
`start = local 00:00 dayStr` → summer `(dayStr)T00:00+01:00` = prior calendar day `T23:00:00.000Z`;
`end = local 00:00 dayStr+1` → summer `dayStr T23:00:00.000Z`;
`createdAt >= start AND createdAt < end`.

(1) N+1 query anti-pattern (serial per-id awaits).

(2) For N routines you run 1 + N (or 2N) round-trips. Latency and DB load grow linearly with N; the loop never batches, so connection/time cost compounds under load.

(3) Bound to a few queries: load all routines and recent runs in bulk, then group in memory.

```ts
async function loadRoutineCards(routineIds: string[]) {
  if (!routineIds.length) return [];
  const routineRows = await db.query.routines.findMany({
    where: inArray(routines.id, routineIds),
  });
  const runRows = await db.query.runs.findMany({
    where: inArray(runs.routineId, routineIds),
    orderBy: [desc(runs.createdAt)], // or your sort
  });
  const runsByRoutine = new Map<string, typeof runRows>();
  for (const r of runRows) {
    const list = runsByRoutine.get(r.routineId) ?? [];
    if (list.length < 5) list.push(r);
    runsByRoutine.set(r.routineId, list);
  }
  return routineRows.map((routine) => ({
    routine,
    runs: runsByRoutine.get(routine.id) ?? [],
  }));
}
```

Prefer a window/lateral SQL (`ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY routine_id) <= 5`) if the runs table is large.

(1) SQL injection via unsanitized string interpolation of `req.query.name`. Exploit input: `' OR '1'='1' --`

(2) Worst case on a shared multi-OpCo DB: read or modify any tenant’s routines (and, with stacked queries/UNION, other tables)—full cross-tenant data breach, tampering, or destruction.

(3) Use a bound parameter, not concatenation:

```ts
const rows = await db.execute(
  `SELECT id, schedule, last_run FROM routines WHERE name = $1`,
  [name]
);
```

Why: the driver sends SQL and values separately; Postgres never treats `name` as SQL syntax, so quotes/operators in input cannot alter the query structure.
